  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In response to the devastating impact of the COVID-19 pandemic that resulted in the loss of income and livelihoods by migrant domestic workers in South Africa, the Izwi Domestic Workers Alliance and the Disabled Migrants&apos; Rights Networking Organization piloted a  cash transfer initiative for  vulnerable migrant domestic workers resident in South Africa in June 2020.  This pilot initiative provided some income relief to the 900 migrant domestic workers who had experienced the devastating loss of jobs and livelihoods as a consequence of the pandemic. The Cash transfers initiative to domestic workers had a positive impact in terms of alleviating the distress and extreme poverty that many had become subjected to as a result of the pandemic. Interviews held with the beneficiaries of the cash income transfers revealed that many of the beneficiaries were able to buy food and medication and to support their children during periods when Government had instituted tough lockdown measures to curb the spread of the COVID-19 pandemic. With support of the European Union, the ILO initiated a partnership with existing membership based organizations representing both local and migrant domestic Workers in South Africa to facilitate a pilot cash transfer initiative targeting vulnerable migrant domestic workers.  The ILO undertook an audit of registered unions and organizations representing domestic workers that potentially could be partnered with to facilitate cash transfers to the vulnerable domestic workers.  The ILO established short agreements with Izwi Domestic Workers Alliance and the Disable Migrants&apos; Rights Networking Organization, given that both organisations had made requests to the ILO for support to migrant domestic workers.  

Critical for sustainability of income relief to vulnerable domestic workers is that the organizations with which the ILO collaborated were already providing support services to domestic workers. This augurs well for sustainability of income relief to vulnerable domestic workers during natural disasters and humanitarian crisis.  Some of the organizations had already begun projects to enhance the resilience of vulnerable domestic workers in times of humanitarian crises.</narrative>
   </description>
